How salty is 
@michaeljburry
 that he missed out on the greatest technology since the Internet? After predicting and profiting from the 2008 subprime mortgage crisis (personally earning around $100 million while generating $700-800 million for his investors through Scion Capital), Michael Burry closed his hedge fund in 2008 and largely stepped away from public investing until relaunching Scion Asset Management in 2013. He invested portions of his post-2008 profits into farmland (focusing on water-rich areas as a proxy for water scarcity plays) and gold, viewing both as hedges against inflation and economic instability. He has described farmland as a sustainable way to capitalize on water redistribution, growing food in water-abundant regions for sale in drier ones. Burry has also maintained a long-term interest in gold as a store of value during periods of monetary disorder or excessive money supply growth. As of the latest available 13F filing for Scion Asset Management (Q3 2025, before he deregistered and shut down the fund in November 2025), Burry's main exposures were in U.S. equities, with a concentrated long portfolio valued at around $52-68 million across just four positions (high turnover rate of 41-58%). These included: Healthcare: Molina Healthcare (MOH) at 35% of the portfolio ($23.9 million, 125,000 shares; a new position in Q3). Consumer discretionary: Lululemon Athletica (LULU) at 26% ($17.8 million, 100,000 shares; doubled his stake in Q3). Financials: SLM Corp (SLM, student loans) at 19.5% ($13.3 million, 480,000 shares; new position). Preferred stock: Bruker Corp (BRKRP.PFD, 6.375% convertible) at 19% ($13.1 million; new position). However, the fund's total managed securities were ~$1.38 billion, with ~80% in bearish put options—primarily a $912 million notional put on AI software firm Palantir (PLTR, 5 million shares equivalent) and a $187 million notional put on Nvidia (NVDA). This reflects a contrarian, value-oriented approach with heavy short exposure to overhyped AI sectors. He exited prior positions in names like Estee Lauder (EL), UnitedHealth (UNH), Regeneron (REGN), Alibaba (BABA), and Meta (META) during Q3. Since shutting down Scion, his current personal portfolio isn't publicly disclosed, but his historical patterns and recent X posts/Substack suggest ongoing focus on undervalued stocks (e.g., he has discussed GameStop/GME as a potential $100 billion opportunity via capital deployment in cheap markets) and macro hedges. Burry has historically been "deep" in gold as a personal hedge (post-2008 investments emphasized it alongside farmland), but no public depth figures exist. His recent Substack/X commentary links crypto declines to forced liquidations in tokenized gold/silver futures (estimating up to $1 billion sold off in late January 2026 due to Bitcoin's slide), viewing Bitcoin as speculative "worth nothing" rather than a gold substitute. Bonds: No significant exposure mentioned in recent portfolios or commentary; his focus has been equities and macro bets. Stocks: Heavy involvement, as detailed above—concentrated longs in a few sectors, plus large short bets via options. He frequently rotates (e.g., past bets against Tesla/TSLA, GameStop/GME shorts in 2021 that turned long). Recent X posts highlight value plays like Molina (comparing it to GEICO's 1970s turnaround) and bearish AI views. Land: Post-2008, he went deep into productive farmland with on-site water, treating it as his primary "water" investment (depth unknown, but described as a "good amount" of his capital). Overall, his portfolio avoids crypto-heavy or fintech disruptors, favoring traditional value plays. Via 
@grok

